#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"}] :question/id #uuid "ebb8f782-2616-52d1-8e6a-6c7049f9f9f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-21890" :question/problem "Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds) in the circuit shown in the diagram above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/0caeb32e2a764272e2f5bf55ed6d3cb3.png)\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance (in Farads).*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\cos(3t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $18\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac1{48}\\,\\textrm{F}\n$$\n\nand an inductor of inductance $6\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=18\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 18I = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/48)} = 48q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 6\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n9\\cos(3t) = 18\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 48q + 6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $6$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 8q = \\dfrac32\\cos(3t)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119470 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "8ac6f533-446a-5d8c-863d-eb220e3dca77" :question/math-academy-id "e-21891" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $10\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistance of $30\\,\\Omega$, a capacitance of $\\dfrac{1}{20}\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductance of $L=10\\,\\textrm{H}$. Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The capacitor charge and voltage satisfy $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is given below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/4431ec0a1bf6b5a3df4df83b5784986b.png)\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{(1/20)} = 20q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n10 = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 1\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t \\to \\infty$. In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} = 0\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n(0)+ 3(0) + 2q = 1\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n2q = 1 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad q = \\dfrac12\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged, and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac12\n$$\n\ncoulombs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119471 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/id #uuid "f5902e80-c98b-599c-bd43-80075e7cc53c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21892" :question/problem "Find, in coulombs, the amplitude of the steady-state charge for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/07e20c56c0ebb5cdabe4f739d573ddc9.png)\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_C$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $20\\sin(4t)\\,\\textrm{V}$, a resistor of resistance $30\\,\\Omega$, a capacitor of capacitance $0.05\\,\\textrm{F}$, and an inductor of inductance $10\\,\\textrm{H}$.\n\nThe current $I$, measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$, measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=30\\,\\Omega$. So,\n$V_R = 30I = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$.\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_C$. Therefore,\n$V_C = \\dfrac{q}{C} = \\dfrac{q}{0.05} = 20q$.\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L = 10\\,\\textrm{H}$. Hence,\n$V_L = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\left(\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\right) = 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}$.\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n20\\sin(4t) = 30\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 20q + 10\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q = 2\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution $q_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t^2} + 3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q_c}{\\mathrm{d}t} + 2q_c = 0$.\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n$q_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t}$,\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $4t$. So, we assume that the particular solution is also a sinusoidal with argument $4t$, i.e.,\n$q_p(t) = \\alpha\\cos(4t) + \\beta\\sin(4t)$.To find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, we substitute\n$q_{p}|= α\\cos (4t) + β\\sin (4t),; \\frac{dq_{p}}{dt}|=-4α\\sin (4t) + 4β\\cos (4t),; \\frac{d^{2}q_{p}}{dt^{2}}|=-16α\\cos (4t) - 16β\\sin (4t)$,\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n$(-16α\\cos (4t) - 16β\\sin (4t)) + 3(-4α\\sin (4t) + 4β\\cos (4t)); + 2(α\\cos (4t) + β\\sin (4t))|= 2\\sin (4t); (-14α + 12β)\\cos (4t) + (-12α - 14β)\\sin (4t)|= 2\\sin (4t)$.Equating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n${-14α + 12β = 0\\begin{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\cos (4t)) \\\\ -12α - 14β = 2\\end{vmatrix}(equating the coefficients of\\sin (4t))$Solving this system gives $\\alpha = -\\dfrac{6}{85}$ and $\\beta = -\\dfrac{7}{85}$. Therefore, the particular solution is\n$q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)$.\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nq(t) = q_c(t) + q_p(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t} - \\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $q_c(t) = Ae^{-t} + Be^{-2t}$ is the *transient charge*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)$ is the *steady-state charge*. It governs the charge's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nq(t)\\approx q_p(t) = -\\dfrac{6}{85}\\cos(4t) - \\dfrac{7}{85}\\sin(4t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state charge, in coulombs, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{6}{85}\\right)^2 + \\left(-\\dfrac{7}{85}\\right)^2} \\approx 0.11\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119472 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "47014cdb-9eac-5a82-996c-6ec26344f46f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Expression for the Current"}] :question/id #uuid "c2070894-8653-56e6-af9b-c683b053adc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21893" :question/problem "The charge $q(t)$ on the capacitor, in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit at time $t$ seconds is\n$q(t) = 4e^{-3t} - 2e^{-t} + \\dfrac5{17}\\cos(2t) + \\dfrac3{17}\\sin(2t)$.\n\nFind, in amperes, the exact amplitude of the steady-state **current** $I$ in this circuit." :question/worked-solution "The current $I$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the given expression for $q(t)$, we obtain the following expression for $I$, in amperes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI(t) &= \\frac{d}{dt}(4e^{-3t} - 2e^{-t} + \\frac{5}{17}\\cos (2t) + \\frac{3}{17}\\sin (2t)) \\\\\n&=-12e^{-3t} + 2e^{-t} - \\frac{10}{17}\\sin (2t) + \\frac{6}{17}\\cos (2t)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity $I_c(t) = -12e^{-3t} + 2e^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The function $I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to\\infty$, we have\n\n$$\nI(t)\\approx I_p(t) = -\\dfrac{10}{17}\\sin(2t) + \\dfrac{6}{17}\\cos(2t)\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\left(-\\dfrac{10}{17}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{6}{17}\\right)^2} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{136}{289}} = \\bbox[4pt,border: 1px solid lightgray]{\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{34}}{17}}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3e5f77be-ef08-568f-8c7c-cae99d23beed" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adf36921-5547-5002-b1e4-7cd9f5cf58e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}q}{dt^{2}} + 2\\frac{dq}{dt} + \\frac{1}{10}q = \\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118061} {:answer/id #uuid "04c80454-4426-51a2-b9d7-5bddee8597e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}q}{dt^{2}} + 2\\frac{dq}{dt} + 10q = 12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118062} {:answer/id #uuid "0b16cdff-b09a-5c01-a6a3-dc0c1f49a1cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}q}{dt^{2}} + 2\\frac{dq}{dt} + 10q = \\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118063} {:answer/id #uuid "59638c39-d71f-5c04-a393-80edf12997bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}q}{dt^{2}} + 2\\frac{dq}{dt} + 10q = \\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118064} {:answer/id #uuid "6933843b-4730-5964-956f-e257d4d47103" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}q}{dt^{2}} + \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{dq}{dt} + 10q = \\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59638c39-d71f-5c04-a393-80edf12997bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d^{2}q}{dt^{2}} + 2\\frac{dq}{dt} + 10q = \\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118064} :answer-field/id #uuid "92f58ece-bd49-51e5-9ee6-b197125b64bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118060}] :question/id #uuid "22c646ac-7d01-5a7e-9969-fc276ffea8c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-343714" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $12V$, a resistance of $40Ω$, a capacitance of $0.005F$, and an inductance of $L = 20H$. Find the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ (in coulombs) on the capacitor at time $t$ (in seconds).\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5db2c6fe-1b26-5daa-b281-d6f6481dc268" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ca36a0c-6f1f-5535-a758-4123e663bd74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118079} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d2b5d9-0148-5538-a414-04ea0e9cde86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118080} {:answer/id #uuid "d613c579-0122-5427-a308-831f6664d168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.54" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118081} {:answer/id #uuid "3e56e995-2bdf-54ff-b7bc-214a7bfb25eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118082} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad10b5e-a125-5fed-81af-a0629a78b7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7d2b5d9-0148-5538-a414-04ea0e9cde86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118080} :answer-field/id #uuid "826e4402-c3df-5a00-a850-ee796fa83251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186