jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/q-343948.png)\n\nFind, in amperes, the amplitude of the steady state current for the RCL circuit shown above, rounded to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120274 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "d14944ef-5041-589e-969b-5cb688551545" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118129}] :question/id #uuid "f22963ff-b55b-5a8b-88e3-83527086fce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-345014" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9V$, a resistance of $34Ω$, a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{24}F$, and an inductance of $L = 12H$. Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120275 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "f7fa2061-0ef2-5534-92dd-405ea97cd1dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186118130}] :question/id #uuid "c0e3062a-d545-5008-96ee-4ac928ce7543" :question/math-academy-id "q-345015" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1845/Source/Images/q-345015.png)\n\nFind the steady-state charge for the circuit given in the diagram above.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop $V_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q = CV_{C}$, where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"solving-an-equation-governing-the-charge-in-an-rcl-circuit-sinusoidal-emf\"></a>"}]}
